---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../../../components/docs/Code.astro';
import DeviceConstraints from '../../../components/docs/DeviceConstraints.astro';
import { page } from '../../../data/docs-nav';

const entry = page('/docs/destinations/mac/');

const probe = `# Point a browser or curl at the Mac, on the same network:
curl http://your-mac.local:54330/

{"service":"hozz-receiver","ready":true,"name":"Brandon's Mac"}`;
---

<DocsLayout page={entry} section="Destinations">
  <div class="fact-strip">
    <div>
      <dt>Format</dt>
      <dd>NDJSON</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Port</dt>
      <dd>54330</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Needs</dt>
      <dd>Both devices on one network</dd>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The Mac app is a local receiver and browser for what the phone sends. It accepts batches over
    your network, stores them in a SQLite database on that Mac, charts them, and can expose them to
    an AI assistant through <a href="/docs/mcp/">the MCP server</a>. It is the only destination that
    gives you somewhere to actually <em>look</em> at the data without setting anything else up.
  </p>

  <h2>Setting it up</h2>

  <p>
    The setup is built so that you never type an address or copy a token.
  </p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Open Hozz on the Mac</h3>
      <p>
        It starts a receiver on port <strong>54330</strong> and advertises itself as{' '}
        <code>_hozz._tcp</code> on the local network. Leave it running.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>On the iPhone, add a destination</h3>
      <p>
        The Mac appears at the top of the list under <strong>Your computers</strong>, before the
        five presets. Tap it.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>That is the whole setup</h3>
      <p>
        Hozz fills in the address and the token and saves a working destination. Use{' '}
        <strong>Send a test</strong> to confirm, then watch the Mac's <strong>Activity</strong> tab
        show it arrive.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How the phone finds the Mac</h2>

  <p>There are three routes, tried in order, because each fails in a different situation:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Bonjour.</strong> The Mac advertises <code>_hozz._tcp</code>; the phone browses for
      it. This is the normal path.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your own iCloud Keychain.</strong> The Mac publishes a receiver record through a
      shared Keychain access group. Two devices signed into the same Apple ID are already known to
      belong to the same person, which is far stronger evidence than anything that could be
      established over a home network — so if this is available, the token is used directly with no
      pairing step.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A local sweep.</strong> If mDNS is blocked and no shared record exists, the phone
      sweeps a private <code>/24</code> on port 54330. The port is known, so the network can simply
      be asked.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Every remembered address is probed before it is offered or reused, so a computer that does not
    answer is shown as <strong>Offline — open Hozz on it</strong> rather than saved as a dead
    destination and quietly failing later.
  </p>

  <h2>Authentication</h2>

  <p>
    The receiver requires a token on every delivery, sent in the <code>Authorization</code> header.
    Exactly one route is reachable without it — <code>POST /pair</code> — because handing the token
    over is that route's entire purpose. A wrong or missing token gets <code>401</code>, and the
    attempt is recorded in the Activity tab.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <code>GET</code> to the port answers with a small JSON identification rather than an error, so
    that a browser pointed at it tells you "wrong address" and "not running" apart:
  </p>

  <Code caption="Checking the receiver is up" code={probe} />

  <Callout kind="note" title="Already paired with another device">
    <p>
      A Mac that is already paired answers a second pairing attempt with <code>403</code> and this
      message: <em
        >This Mac is already paired with another device. Open Hozz on the Mac and forget it first,
        or copy the token by hand.</em
      >
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>If the local network will not cooperate</h2>

  <p>
    Some networks refuse inbound connections between devices — guest Wi-Fi, client isolation,
    corporate networks. The Mac app also <strong>watches a folder</strong> for automatic batch
    files, which routes around this entirely.
  </p>

  <p>
    Point the phone at a synced folder, pick the same folder on the Mac, and the Mac ingests new
    NDJSON, JSON, CSV or Metrics JSON files as they arrive. See{' '}
    <a href="/docs/destinations/folder/">Folder</a>. Line protocol is deliberately not offered for
    folders, because the watcher ignores it and a folder writing line protocol would look like it
    was working while nothing was ingested.
  </p>

  <h2>What the Mac app does with it</h2>

  <p>
    Received batches go into <code>hozz-received.sqlite</code> (schema version 7) with idempotent
    batch records, deletions, characteristics, per-device "last heard from" state, and dedicated
    tables for ECGs, audiograms, State of Mind, medication doses and workout statistics.
  </p>

  <p>The app has four tabs:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Connect</strong> — the receiver, the folder watcher, the token, and per-device status based on when data last actually arrived.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Data</strong> — what has arrived and how far back it reaches, your own characteristics
      above the measurements, charts by hour/day/week/month, CSV export of a type, and the name of
      anything received in a form this version cannot read yet.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Assistant</strong> — the MCP configuration to copy into an assistant.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Activity</strong> — recent accepted, duplicate, paired, test and rejected deliveries,
      without logging sample values.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Callout kind="note" title="Quarantine, and why nothing is dropped">
    <p>
      A record the current parser cannot read is not discarded — it goes to a quarantine table and
      is named in the Data tab. When a newer version can read it, a promotion pass adds it properly
      and the UI says it did. A newer phone talking to an older Mac therefore loses nothing.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>A dependency-free alternative</h2>

  <p>
    If you would rather not run the Mac app, a small standalone Python receiver lives in{' '}
    <code>receiver/</code> in the repository. It is a plain script with no dependencies, and it is a
    working example of the protocol if you are writing your own —{' '}
    <a href="/docs/destinations/web/">Your own endpoint</a> documents the headers.
  </p>

  <DeviceConstraints />
</DocsLayout>
